  "text": "I could not verify any of the three answers, so I can't confirm one as correct.\n\n**Solution 3 (answer 2) is wrong.** It contradicts itself: it lists candidate values, then discards some, then cites an \"IMC 2021 solution\" and a set with a different sum. Its values (for example 1, 2/3, 2/5) also don't match the classical 3-chromatic thresholds it claims to build on.\n\n**Solutions 1 and 2 share a sound upper bound and differ in the value set.** Minimum degree above 4/5·n forces K₆(t) (Erdős–Stone). Any 2-colouring of K₆(t) has a monochromatic K₃(t), which contains any 3-chromatic H_i. So every pair's threshold is at most 4/5, and both answers respect this.\n\n- **Solution 1** gives {2/3, 5/7, 3/4, 4/5}. Its first family, (2−d)/(3−2d) for d ∈ {0, 1/3, 1/2}, is a plausible extrapolation. Its 4/5 pentagon construction is not checked, and its remark that (K₃,K₃) matches the n/3 triangle-free result is confused.\n- **Solution 2** adds 7/9 to that set and explicitly says its value set is extrapolated without a matching upper bound. Its 7/9 and 5/7 gadgets are not verified either.\n\nNeither set is backed by a source I can check, and the problem's answer depends on exactly which values occur. Since I can't establish that either sum is right, I won't pick one.\n\nCHOICE: NONE",
